). **You may ONLY create/modify `translation/tests/t_pure.rs`.** Do NOT touch `translation/src/**`, `translation/tests/common/**`, `c_src/**`, `Cargo.toml`, or any other test file.\n\n## Existing harness you MUST use (read it first: `translation/tests/common/mod.rs` and `translation/tests/common/api.rs`)\n\n`mod common;` gives you:\n- `common::libs() -> &'static Libs` with fields `.c` and `.rs` (both `libloading::Library`).\n- `common::both(|lib| ...) -> (R, R)` runs the closure against the C lib then the Rust lib.\n- `common::api::<png_function_name>(lib, args...)` — thin `dlsym` wrappers for ~300 libpng entry points. First arg is always `&libloading::Library`. If a function you need is missing, add a **local** `crate::decl_api! { fn png_foo(a: T) -> R; }` block inside your own file (inside a `mod myapi { use super::common::*; ... }`) — do NOT edit `tests/common/api.rs`.\n- Error capture: `common::rec_error` / `common::rec_warning` are `png_error_ptr`-compatible callbacks; `rec_error` records the message and then panics (standing in for the app's `longjmp`). `common::capture(|| ...) -> Run<T>` / `capture_strict` catch that unwind; `Run { out: Option<T>, log: Vec<Msg> }`; `Msg::Err(Vec<u8>)` / `Msg::Warn(Vec<u8>)`. `Run::assert_eq(&other, \"label\")` compares log + output.\n- `common::api::new_writer(lib)` / `new_reader(lib)` create a png_struct with those handlers installed (you probably only need them where a `png_structp` is required).\n- `common::Rng::new(seed)` — deterministic xorshift RNG: `.u32()`, `.u8()`, `.below(n)`, `.range_i32(lo,hi)`, `.bytes(n)`, `.f64_unit()`.\n- All libpng structs (`png_color`, `png_color_8`, `png_color_16`, `png_time`, `png_row_info`, `png_image`, …) and constants (`PNG_LIBPNG_VER_STRING`, `PNG_COLOR_TYPE_*`, `PNG_FILTER_*`, `PNG_NUMBER_FORMAT_*`, `PNG_fp_MAX`, `PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_SIZE`, …) are in `common`.\n\nLook at `translation/tests/smoke.rs` for a complete working example.\n\n## Your task: create `translation/tests/t_pure.rs`\n\nWrite ONE `#[test]` fn per row below. Each test must call BOTH libraries through their `.so` exports on the SAME inputs and assert byte-identical results (return values, all out-parameters, and the recorded warning/error message log). Use MANY randomized inputs per row (fixed seeds, e.g. `Rng::new(0xC0FFEE + row)`), at least a few hundred iterations where cheap, plus the hand-picked boundary values listed.\n\nRows (from `translation/CONFIGS.md`):\n1. `png_access_version_number`, `png_get_libpng_ver`, `png_get_header_ver`, `png_get_header_version`, `png_get_copyright` — compare numbers and the full C strings (use `CStr::from_ptr`).\n2. `png_save_uint_32`/`png_save_uint_16`/`png_save_int_32` into a 4-byte buffer and `png_get_uint_32`/`png_get_uint_16`/`png_get_int_32` reading it back: 4096 random u32 plus 0, 1, 0x7fffffff, 0x80000000, 0xffffffff.\n3. `png_get_uint_31(pp, buf)` for random u32 <= 0x7fffffff (valid path only). Needs a png_structp — use `new_reader`.\n4. `png_muldiv(&mut res, a, times, divisor)` — random i32 for all three (full range) plus 0 divisor, ±1, i32::MIN/MAX; compare BOTH the return value and `res`.\n5. `png_reciprocal(a)`, `png_reciprocal2(a,b)` — random i32 incl. 0.\n6. `png_gamma_significant(g)`, `png_gamma_8bit_correct(v,g)` (v in 0..=255), `png_gamma_16bit_correct(v,g)` (v in 0..=65535), `png_gamma_correct(pp,v,g)` — random g in 1..=1000000 plus 100000, 45455, 220000, 1, 1000000.\n7. `png_build_gamma_table(pp, bit_depth)` then `png_destroy_gamma_table(pp)` for bit_depth 8 and 16. NOTE: this needs gamma fields set on png_struct first — set them through the public API by calling `png_set_gamma_fixed(pp, screen, file)` on a reader **and** `png_set_alpha_mode_fixed` as needed; if you cannot observe the generated tables through public exports (there is no getter), then instead verify the *observable* effect: do the differential test of gamma through a full read (skip building tables directly) OR simply assert that both libraries do/don't emit the same warnings and don't crash for the same inputs. Document in a comment what you verified.\n8. Exported data arrays `png_sRGB_table` (256 u16), `png_sRGB_base` (512 u16), `png_sRGB_delta` (512 u8): resolve them as data symbols with `lib.get::<*const u16>(b\"png_sRGB_table\\0\")` (note: a data symbol resolves to the address OF the array, so cast the `Symbol` to `*const u16` via `.into_raw()`/deref carefully — simplest: `let s: libloading::Symbol<*const u16> = lib.get(b\"png_sRGB_table\\0\").unwrap(); let p = *s;` is WRONG for arrays; use `let s: libloading::Symbol<u16> = ...; let p = s.into_raw().into_raw() as *const u16;` or `lib.get::<()>()`… figure out a correct approach and byte-compare the full arrays between the two libraries).\n9. `png_XYZ_from_xy` / `png_xy_from_XYZ`. The C types are `png_XYZ { png_fixed_point red_X, red_Y, red_Z, green_X, green_Y, green_Z, blue_X, blue_Y, blue_Z; }` and `png_xy { png_fixed_point whitex, whitey, redx, redy, greenx, greeny, bluex, bluey; }` (check `c_src/include/pngpriv.h` to confirm field order!). Define `#[repr(C)]` structs locally. Test sRGB primaries (whitex=31270, whitey=32900, redx=64000, redy=33000, greenx=30000, greeny=60000, bluex=15000, bluey=6000) plus random fixed values; compare return code and all output fields.\n10. `png_check_fp_number(s, size, &mut state, &mut whereami)` and `png_check_fp_string(s, size)`: hand-picked strings `\"1\"`, `\"-.5e+10\"`, `\"0x\"`, `\"1e\"`, `\"..\"`, `\"\"`, `\"+\"`, `\"1.5E-3\"`, `\"00\"`, `\"1.\"`, `\".5\"`, `\"e5\"`, plus 500 random 1..12-char strings drawn from the alphabet `0123456789+-.eE ` . Initialise `state` to 0 and `whereami` to 0 and compare both after the call, as well as the return value.\n11. `png_ascii_from_fp(pp, buf, PNG_fp_MAX, value, precision)` — buffer `[c_char; 32]`, precision 1..=15, values 0.0, 1.0, -1.0, 1e-10, 1e10, f64::MIN_POSITIVE, f64::MAX, plus 500 randoms (`rng.f64_unit() * 10f64.powi(rng.range_i32(-12,12))`, both signs). Compare the whole 32-byte buffer.\n12. `png_ascii_from_fixed(pp, buf, PNG_fp_MAX, fp)` — random i32 incl. i32::MIN, i32::MAX, 0, ±1. Compare the whole buffer.\n13. `png_fixed(pp, d, \"x\")` and `png_fixed_ITU(pp, d, \"x\")` for in-range doubles only (|d| < 21474 for png_fixed; check the C in `c_src/src/png.c`) — compare return value and message log.\n14. `png_safecat(buf, bufsize, pos, str)`, `png_format_number(start, end, format, number)`, and the warning-parameter trio + `png_formatted_warning`. For `png_format_number` allocate `[c_char; PNG_NUMBER_BUFFER_SIZE]`, pass `start = buf.as_ptr()`, `end = buf.as_mut_ptr().add(PNG_NUMBER_BUFFER_SIZE)` and compare the returned pointer OFFSET (not the absolute pointer) plus the buffer contents; formats 1,2,3,4,5. For the warning parameters use `[c_char; PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_SIZE * PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_COUNT]` zeroed (check `pngpriv.h` for the real `png_warning_parameters` type — it is `char [PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_COUNT][PNG_WARNING_PARAMETER_SIZE]`) and compare the recorded warning message text emitted by `png_formatted_warning` for messages containing `@1`…`@8`.\n15. `png_convert_to_rfc1123_buffer(out29, &t)` and `png_convert_to_rfc1123(pp, &t)` and `png_convert_from_time_t(&mut t, secs)`: all 12 months, day 1 and 31, hour 0/23, minute 0/59, second 0/60, plus 300 random `png_time`s (including out-of-range month 0/13 — those must produce the same rejection in both). `png_convert_from_struct_tm` takes a `*const struct tm`: build a `#[repr(C)] struct Tm { tm_sec: c_int, tm_min: c_int, tm_hour: c_int, tm_mday: c_int, tm_mon: c_int, tm_year: c_int, tm_wday: c_int, tm_yday: c_int, tm_isdst: c_int, /* glibc: */ tm_gmtoff: c_long, tm_zone: *const c_char }`.\n16. `png_sig_cmp(sig, start, num)` — correct 8-byte signature `[137,80,78,71,13,10,26,10]`, every single-byte corruption, all `start` 0..=8 × `num` 0..=8.\n17. `png_check_IHDR(pp, w, h, bd, ct, il, cm, ft)` — cross product w ∈ {0,1,7,8,1000000,1000001,0x80000000} × h same × bd ∈ 0..=17 × ct ∈ 0..=7 × il ∈ 0..=2 × cm ∈ {0,1} × ft ∈ {0,1}. That is too many; sample it: iterate all bd×ct×il×cm×ft with a couple of (w,h) pairs, plus a random sample of 3000 full combinations. `png_check_IHDR` calls `png_error` in some branches, so wrap each call in `common::capture(...)` and compare `out.is_some()` and the whole `log`.\n18. `png_build_grayscale_palette(bit_depth, palette)` with a `[png_color; 256]` buffer, bit_depth ∈ {0,1,2,3,4,8,16} — compare all 256 entries.\n\n## Rules\n- Never call a Rust function directly; always go through `libs().rs` via the `api::` wrappers or your own `decl_api!`.\n- Wrap every call that could hit `png_error` in `common::capture` (it will otherwise abort the test process).\n- Note `png_error` handlers are per-`png_struct`; create a fresh struct per capture where an error may destroy it.\n- The C is ALWAYS correct. If C and Rust differ, that is a Rust bug: **do NOT fix it, do NOT weaken the assertion, do NOT `#[ignore]` the test.** Leave the test failing and report the exact divergence.\n- Compile & run with, from `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-SS8HRb/translation`:\n  `timeout 600 cargo test --offline --release --test t_pure 2>&1 | tail -40`\n  (`--offline` is mandatory: there is no network. `--release` is mandatory: the debug profile enables overflow checks that the C does not have.)\n- Iterate until the file COMPILES with no errors (compile errors in your file are your responsibility).\n\n## Report back\n1. The list of rows you covered and the exact `#[test]` name for each.\n2. For every FAILING test: the exact divergence (input, C output, Rust output, message logs).\n3. Anything you could not test and why.","outputFile":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-6wP1XG/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-SS8HRb/814b2895-9042-4f5f-bd73-35bea46615cb/tasks/a34897d0ff6cf8ff7.output","canReadOutputFile":true}}
