{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nn226oekf5repxafuyc5j3lnbduk5srpktkts4qp5egcdqj6nkbq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 10 tests compile cleanly and **pass** — no C↔Rust divergence found in any of the row operations.\n\n```\nrunning 10 tests ... test result: ok. 10 passed; 0 failed (0.71s)\n```\n\nFile: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-SS8HRb/translation/tests/t_rowops.rs` (833 lines, only file created/modified).\n\n## 1. Coverage: row → test name\n\n| Row | `#[test]` | Cases (each run against both `.so`s) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 19 | `r19_do_bgr`, `r19_do_invert`, `r19_do_swap`, `r19_do_packswap` | 3 500 each = `ct{0,2,3,4,6} × bd{1,2,4,8,16} × w{1,2,3,7,8,9,33} × 20` random rows (incl. impossible combos like bd=1/ct=6) |\n| 20 | `r20_do_strip_channel` | 2 240 = `ct{0,4,2,6}` (channels 1,2,3,4) `× bd{8,16} × at_start{0,1} × 7 widths × 20` |\n| 21 | `r21_do_check_palette_indexes` | 11 valid `(bit_depth, num_palette)` combos × 12 rows × 18–24 synthesised `row_info` probes ≈ 2 800 direct calls, plus the `png_write_end` report path |\n| 22 | `r22_read_filter_row` | 74 520 = `pd{1,2,4,8,16,24,32,48,64} × filter{-1,0,1,2,3,4,5} × rowbytes 1..40 × 30` random `(row, prev_row)` pairs |\n| 23 | `r23_do_read_interlace` | 23 100 = `pass 0..6 × ct{5} × bd{5} × transformations{0, PNG_PACKSWAP=0x10000} × w 1..=33 × 2` |\n| 24 | `r24_do_write_interlace` | 23 100 = `pass 0..6 (6 = no-op) × ct{5} × bd{5} × w 1..=33 × 4` |\n| 25 | `r25_combine_row_via_progressive_adam7` | 7 Adam7 image shapes (GRAY 1/2, RGB 8, RGBA 16, PALETTE 4, GA 8; incl. 1×1 and 3×2 to hit the empty-pass paths) |\n\nEvery case allocates a `rowbytes + 64` (or `PNG_ROWBYTES(pd, final_width) + 64` for row 23) buffer filled with an identical random pattern and compares the **whole** buffer, the mutated `png_row_info`, and the message log / unwind status. Each test also asserts its exact case count so it can't silently become vacuous.\n\n## 2. Failing tests\n\nNone. I verified the harness is not vacuous by temporarily pointing the Rust side of `r19_do_bgr` at `png_do_packswap`; it failed immediately with a full hex dump and byte offset.\n\n## 3. Notes / things I could not test as literally specified\n\n- **Row 21 – `png_do_check_palette_indexes` takes its row from `pp->row_buf`, not from a row argument** (`c_src/src/pngtrans.c:728`), and its only effect is on the private `pp->num_palette_max`. So the row content is established with `png_write_row` on a real palette writer, `png_set_check_for_invalid_index(pp, 1)` resets `num_palette_max` to 0 before each probe, and `png_get_palette_max(pp, ip)` reads it back. The function is then called **directly** with 18–24 synthesised `row_info`s (width/bit_depth/pixel_depth/rowbytes varied, always `rowbytes <= the real row_buf size`). The function itself emits no messages; the observable message is `png_write_end`'s `png_benign_error(\"Wrote palette index exceeding num_palette\")`, which both libraries raise as an unwinding error identically. The probe values are strongly varying (e.g. bd=8/np=1 gives `[0,0,1,1,1,0,0,3,3,3,0,3,5,14,15,0,49,92,238,248,…]`), so the test is discriminating.\n- **Row 22 – 36 of 2 520 shape combos are skipped**: the C `png_read_filter_row_avg` and `png_read_filter_row_paeth_multibyte_pixel` compute `istop = row_info->rowbytes - bpp` in `size_t`, which **underflows and segfaults** when `rowbytes < bpp` (confirmed: `pd=16, filter=3(AVG), rowbytes=1` crashes the *C* library with SIGSEGV). libpng never produces such a `row_info`, so those inputs are out of contract and are `continue`d with a comment. All `rowbytes >= bpp` are covered for all filters; filters `-1, 0, 1, 2, 5` are covered for all `rowbytes 1..40`.\n- **Row 25 – `png_combine_row` cannot be driven standalone** (`pp->row_buf/pass/width/interlaced/transformations/row_info` are all private, with no public setters). It is exercised through its thin public wrapper `png_progressive_combine_row` (`pngpread.c:907` = `if (new_row != NULL) png_combine_row(png_ptr, old_row, 1)`) called from inside the progressive row callback, snapshotting the whole destination buffer after **every** callback and comparing the snapshot sequence, the final image and the full `(row_number, pass)` argument sequence. The info callback installs `png_set_interlace_handling` + `png_read_update_info` (the latter is required, otherwise the progressive IDAT reader dereferences an unallocated `row_buf`). The test PNG is encoded **once with the C writer** and fed to both readers, deliberately isolating the combine/read path from any deflate-output differences between the two libraries (encoder byte-equality belongs to a different config row). A sanity assertion confirms the C round-trip reproduces the original pixels — masking only the padding bits of the last byte of each row, which blocky (`display=1`) combining legitimately fills with replicated pixel data.\n- `png_do_read_interlace` inputs use libpng's real `PNG_ROWBYTES` (which for `pixel_depth >= 8` is `width * (pd>>3)`, i.e. differs from `(w*pd+7)>>3` for `pd=12`), since the function *writes* `rowbytes = PNG_ROWBYTES(pixel_depth, final_width)` and the buffer must be sized to match. Rows 19/20 use the `(w*pd+7)>>3` form given in the task (always ≥ `PNG_ROWBYTES`, so still safe)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":212,"cache_read_input_tokens":131507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":212,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016HQkuVYD96wCMukzNpp6Ei","session_id":"814b2895-9042-4f5f-bd73-35bea46615cb","uuid":"0d1283a4-a136-4de1-b2d0-ef81c1a9d610","timestamp":"2026-09-08T15:32:36.828Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write t_rowops.rs differential tests"}
